# Flink Session Windows (Event-Time)

## Purpose
Group events into "sessions" per key when the time gap between consecutive events exceeds a threshold (e.g., 10 minutes). Useful for stage detection or inactivity-based segmentation.

## Key Ideas
- Use **event time** with a **WatermarkStrategy**.
- Convert timestamps to a single time unit (milliseconds recommended) and use the same unit for the session gap.
- Session windows close when the watermark passes the window end.

## Example (DataStream API)
```java
WatermarkStrategy<MyEvent> wm = WatermarkStrategy
    .<MyEvent>forMonotonousTimestamps()
    .withTimestampAssigner((e, ts) -> e.eventTimeMillis);

DataStream<Tuple2<Long, Integer>> sessionCounts = events
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(wm)
    .keyBy(e -> e.jobId)
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(new CountAgg(), new ToJobCountWindow());
```

## Notes
- If input timestamps are in microseconds, either:
  - Convert to milliseconds (divide by 1000) and use `Time.minutes(10)`, or
  - Keep microseconds and use `Time.milliseconds(600_000_000L)`.
- Session windows handle resubmissions naturally: each SUBMIT event counts.

## Minimal Aggregators
```java
public static class CountAgg implements AggregateFunction<MyEvent, Long, Long> {
  public Long createAccumulator() { return 0L; }
  public Long add(MyEvent e, Long acc) { return acc + 1; }
  public Long getResult(Long acc) { return acc; }
  public Long merge(Long a, Long b) { return a + b; }
}

public static class ToJobCountWindow extends ProcessWindowFunction<Long, Tuple2<Long, Integer>, Long, TimeWindow> {
  public void process(Long key, Context ctx, Iterable<Long> counts, Collector<Tuple2<Long, Integer>> out) {
    long count = counts.iterator().next();
    out.collect(Tuple2.of(key, (int) count));
  }
}
```
